WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.740
A pointer can play several roles, and security review depends on knowing which role is intended.

2
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.520
An owning pointer is responsible for an object's lifetime.

3
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:17.980
In modern C++, raw owning pointers should be avoided in new designs because ownership is easy to miss and hard to audit.

4
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:24.400
Prefer a type such as std::unique_ptr or a focused resource wrapper when code owns something.

5
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:29.720
A borrowed pointer gives temporary access to an object owned elsewhere.

6
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:38.220
Borrowing can be appropriate for optional parameters, legacy APIs, or short call paths where a reference is not the right shape.

7
00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:43.920
The contract must say that the callee does not delete the object and does not store the pointer beyond the valid lifetime.

8
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:48.260
Borrowed access should be brief, local, and easy to trace.

9
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:55.980
An observing pointer or handle may allow code to check or refer to something without controlling lifetime.

10
00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:01.920
Observation appears in caches, registries, callbacks, and object graphs.

11
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:10.340
It can be useful, but it becomes unsafe when observers accidentally keep stale access or when the observed object's owner is not obvious.

12
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:16.360
For shared ownership designs, std::weak_ptr can express observation without extending lifetime.

13
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:23.600
Pointers may also refer to arrays, buffers, output locations, or external resources.

14
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.380
A handle from a C API may require a specific release function.

15
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.320
A pointer-to-pointer parameter may mean the callee writes a result.

16
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.900
These are not interchangeable roles.

17
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:41.040
A safe API makes the role visible through type choice, naming, documentation, and ownership conventions.

18
00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:51.140
Code comments should not be the only place this meaning appears; parameter names, wrapper types, and return types should carry the contract where possible.

19
00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:56.200
During review, ask what the pointer means before reasoning about what the code does.
